"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,928\nThe Learning Brain\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,613\nEpisode 17\n\n3\n00:00:07,693 --> 00:00:09,513\nMetacognition\n\n4\n00:00:12,313 --> 00:00:14,104\nShow people words in big,\n\n5\n00:00:14,143 --> 00:00:15,599\nforty eight point type,\n\n6\n00:00:15,619 --> 00:00:17,629\nor small, eighteen point type,\n\n7\n00:00:17,915 --> 00:00:20,679\nand ask how likely they are to remember each one.\n\n8\n00:00:21,143 --> 00:00:23,605\nThey rate the big words as more memorable.\n\n9\n00:00:23,900 --> 00:00:24,877\nThen test them.\n\n10\n00:00:25,279 --> 00:00:26,741\nRecall does not differ.\n\n11\n00:00:27,170 --> 00:00:30,467\nThe feeling of learning and actual learning came apart.\n\n12\n00:00:30,637 --> 00:00:35,053\nThat feeling comes from fluency: how easy something seems right now.\n\n13\n00:00:35,400 --> 00:00:36,845\nRereading feels fluent.\n\n14\n00:00:37,386 --> 00:00:40,569\nIn a survey of a hundred seventy seven college students,\n\n15\n00:00:40,864 --> 00:00:43,557\neighty four percent said they reread to study,\n\n16\n00:00:43,811 --> 00:00:46,926\nand fifty five percent called it their main strategy.\n\n17\n00:00:47,398 --> 00:00:49,934\nOnly eleven percent tested themselves,\n\n18\n00:00:50,120 --> 00:00:53,139\nand just one percent made that their main strategy.\n\n19\n00:00:53,309 --> 00:00:55,918\nThe illusion can even survive the evidence.\n\n20\n00:00:56,278 --> 00:00:59,180\nPeople learned the painting styles of twelve artists,\n\n21\n00:00:59,312 --> 00:01:00,749\nsome shown in blocks,\n\n22\n00:01:00,948 --> 00:01:03,588\none artist at a time,\n\n23\n00:01:04,129 --> 00:01:05,288\nand some mixed together.\n\n24\n00:01:05,585 --> 00:01:08,388\nMixing worked better: eighty five percent did as well or better with it.\n\n25\n00:01:08,804 --> 00:01:12,425\nYet eighty three percent rated blocking as equal or better,\n\n26\n00:01:12,649 --> 00:01:14,414\neven after their own test.\n\n27\n00:01:14,584 --> 00:01:16,113\nThere is a simple repair.\n\n28\n00:01:16,522 --> 00:01:18,586\nJudge your learning right after studying,\n\n29\n00:01:18,606 --> 00:01:20,722\nand you are reading short term memory.\n\n30\n00:01:21,258 --> 00:01:24,199\nWait, then try to recall before you judge,\n\n31\n00:01:24,271 --> 00:01:26,763\nand predictions become far more accurate.\n\n32\n00:01:27,296 --> 00:01:30,141\nThis is the delayed judgment of learning effect,\n\n33\n00:01:30,371 --> 00:01:33,164\none of the most reliable findings in the field.\n\n34\n00:01:33,333 --> 00:01:41,661\nIn the brain, people who are better at judging their own accuracy tend to have more grey matter in the anterior prefrontal cortex,\n\n35\n00:01:41,874 --> 00:01:42,881\nat the very front.\n\n36\n00:01:43,519 --> 00:01:45,113\nBut this is a correlation,\n\n37\n00:01:45,324 --> 00:01:46,750\nin thirty two people.\n\n38\n00:01:47,179 --> 00:01:50,071\nIt does not show that the region causes the skill,\n\n39\n00:01:50,298 --> 00:01:52,104\nor that the skill is fixed.\n\n40\n00:01:52,274 --> 00:01:53,031\nFor teaching.\n\n41\n00:01:53,428 --> 00:01:56,541\nWarn students that fluent is not the same as learned.\n\n42\n00:01:56,826 --> 00:01:59,813\nHave them predict their score,\n\n43\n00:02:00,288 --> 00:02:02,131\nthen compare it with the result.\n\n44\n00:02:02,151 --> 00:02:03,632\nUse delayed self tests,\n\n45\n00:02:04,145 --> 00:02:05,798\nnot rereading, as the check.\n\n46\n00:02:05,818 --> 00:02:07,839\nAfter exams, ask what they did,\n\n47\n00:02:08,120 --> 00:02:09,538\nand what they will change.\n\n48\n00:02:09,714 --> 00:02:11,273\nAnd model it yourself: say when you are unsure,\n\n49\n00:02:11,293 --> 00:02:12,271\nand how you would check.\n\n50\n00:02:12,441 --> 00:02:14,591\nFeeling that you know is not knowing.\n\n51\n00:02:14,852 --> 00:02:16,250\nTeach students to check.\n\n"
